# RDF Turtle, as the tree-sitter grammar turtle gives it: what each kind of its nodes is in the file's tree. A recipe
# for a Turtle file says "format turtle" and then only what its source's parts are.
#   a triple holds its subject and its properties; a property, its predicate and its objects
#   an IRI is its text without the angle brackets; a prefixed name, as written; a literal holds its text (quotes off,
#   escapes resolved) and its language tag (without the @) or its datatype
#   prefix and base directives, blank nodes and collections are not of the tree
grammar turtle
resolve turtle
node triple group kind
node subject group kind
node property group kind
node predicate group kind
node object_list group kind
node rdf_literal group kind
node iri_reference text
node prefixed_name text
node string text
node lang_tag text kind
node integer text raw
node decimal text raw
node double text raw
node boolean_literal text raw
node directive skip
node prefix_id skip
node base skip
node sparql_prefix skip
node sparql_base skip
node blank_node_property_list skip
node anon skip
node collection skip
node blank_node_label skip
node comment skip
